      "title": "Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032141 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Dalton Nuclear Institute Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/09/2026 Contract Duration: start as soon as possible contract length dependent on start date, post funding expires 31/03/2031 School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Job Description This Research Associate (RA) will form an integral part of the Actinides Centre of Excellence (ACE), which is a collaboration between the University of Manchester and AWE, and has the intention to grow by the inclusion of other national partners and universities. This role represents an exciting opportunity by helping to establish ACE (https://www.awe.co.uk/2026/06/awe-and-the-university-of-manchester-launch-actinides-centre-of-excellence/) - a national hub of collaborative research activity that aims to progress fundamental and low TRL Actinide Science & Innovation, intends to attract and train cohorts of future nuclear talent, and create tomorrow’s Subject Matter Experts. This role is part of a commitment from both the University of Manchester and AWE to advancing actinides research and innovation across thematic areas including materials recycle and solution process chemistry, and will play a pivotal part in shaping our future research landscape in nuclear science and engineering. The RA will be responsible for undertaking experimental research in actinide process chemistry and chemical engineering to assist in the development, optimization and sustainability of advanced actinide recovery processes. This role provides the opportunity to work across multiple projects over the term of the post delivering high priority actinide research needs for AWE as determined by the ACE management team. Experimental research activities are likely to vary from, for example, understanding actinide speciation in a wide range of solution process conditions to undertaking scale up experiments to support the design of advanced actinide recovery processes. Processes of interest may involve (but are not limited to): Dissolution Ion exchange/adsorption Solvent extraction, precipitation Electrodeposition operations; across primarily aqueous systems but could also involve organic and molten salt media. It is expected this research associate will closely engage with the other ACE research focus areas in analysis & spectroscopy and solid state science, and contribute to the growth of ACE activities with the involvement of new partners and expanded research interests. This role, therefore, offers a significant opportunity to develop a wide range of high-level skills and expertise providing a clear pathway to become a highly valued subject matter expert for the nuclear sector. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Clint Sharrad (Deputy Director of the Dalton Nuclear Institute) Email: clint.a.sharrad@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032141 Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry) FP'S (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45422": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions Apply for job Job reference: SAE-031817 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Earth & Environmental Sciences Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/09/2026 Contract Duration: 30 Months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an enthusiastic and talented Post-Doctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to join the Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences at the University of Manchester and work with Filipa Cox and Joshua Lynn to explore plant-mycorrhizal fungal life-history strategies in relation to plant life-history. The project will address the following questions: 1) Do mycorrhizal traits match plant traits along the fast–slow life-history continuum? 2) Do plants and mycorrhizal fungi in symbiosis have complementary traits and, if so, is this driven by shared environmental conditions or by plant and/or fungal partner preferences? You will use a combination of macroecological and experimental techniques, including plant and mycorrhizal functional trait characterisation, molecular characterisation of fungal communities, working with “big data”, and characterising soil physicochemical properties. You will join a diverse, active and collaborative research group in the Soil and Ecosystem Ecology Lab at Manchester. You will be responsible for: Design and establish mesocosm experiments involving plants and mycorrhizal fungi Conduct and organise fieldwork to assess plant and fungal responses to environmental gradients Analyse and interpret resulting data and communicate results via presentations, and write papers for publication in leading journals Communicate research plans and findings with project collaborators and other staff working on the project Undertake knowledge exchange activities to generate wider impact of research We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Have a relevant PhD (or equivalent) in fungal, soil or plant ecology, and experience of field- and/or laboratory-based research Specialist knowledge on plant:soil interactions and fungal ecology Ability to travel to fieldsites in UK and Europe Experience in some/all research methodologies relevant to the post such as analysis of soil organic compounds, microbial community analyses, techniques to manipulate mycorrhizal fungal interactions and functioning, use of stable isotopes and enzyme assays Experience in analysis and interpretation of experimental data, including statistical analyses of large datasets, preferably from studies done at large spatial scales Desirable Criteria: Specific research experience in mycorrhizal fungal ecology Experience in the synthesis of plant-mycorrhizal symbioses in the laboratory Experience with species distribution modelling UK Driving licence We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Filipa Cox and Joshua Lynn Email Address: filipa.cox@manchester.ac.uk and joshua.lynn@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-031817 Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions Further Particulars (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45922": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032265 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a highly motivated and enthusiastic Postdoctoral Research Associate with an interest in human plasma proteomics to join a pioneering project exploring the links between genetics and hypertension. This post creates a unique opportunity to generate high quality plasma proteomics data on a large patient cohort using mass spectrometry approaches. The postholder will then analyse these data, including appropriate quality control, and work with a wider team to integrate these data with additional relevant measurements, including genotypic and clinical data. The successful candidate will play an important role in developing and demonstrating the effectiveness of mass spectrometry-based proteomics at scale for human cohort studies, including the potential for large-scale multi-omics research for determining mechanisms underlying disease, such as protein-wide association studies. Excellent technical and organisational skills are essential, along with a passion for collaborative, data-driven research that contributes to improving people’s health. You will be responsible for: •    To take initiative in the planning of research and drive forward the progress of the project. •    To retain responsibility for the appropriate organization, storage and management of research samples, including Human Tissue Act-relevant material where appropriate. •    To plan and execute protein extraction and preparation protocols and liaise with colleagues for data generation. •    To conduct data analysis using a range of software and statistical tools. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: •    Hold (or expect to hold shortly) a PhD in a relevant area of research •    Extensive and up-to-date theoretical and practical knowledge in mass spectrometry and/or protein quantitation •    Experience of mass spectrometry analysis of human-derived samples from large cohort studies, with proteomics experience strongly preferred. •    Experience in analysis and quality control assessment of mass spectrometry-based proteomics data Desirable Criteria: •    Experience in interpreting disease-related proteomics data, in particular processes related to age-related diseases or vascular health •    Experience in data analysis using DIA-NN •    Experience in pathway analysis and enrichment software, including but not limited to STRING, Reactome. •    Experience in establishing data analysis workflows and scripts in R. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: •    Generous employer contribution pension •    29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure •    Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Richard Unwin Email Address: r.unwin@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars with person specification linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032265 - Research Associate - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45916": {
      "title": "Research Project Coordinator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Project Coordinator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Project Coordinator Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032261 Salary: £33,002 to £33,951 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 11/09/2026 Contract Duration: 36 Months in first instance School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an experienced, motivated and proactive Research Project Coordinator to work on the YouChoose Study. This role offers an exciting opportunity to work on a programme which will evaluate the NHS cervical screening programmes in England and Wales to determine if offering the choice of self- or clinician sampling is feasible, acceptable and improves user experience above the standard of care, effective at increasing screening participation, able to reduce health inequalities, effective for CIN2+ detection, and good value for money. YouChoose is sponsored by The University of Manchester. The post holder should have excellent communication and interpersonal skills. You should have some knowledge and an understanding of research governance processes within academic and/ or NHS settings and be able to manage busy workloads and prioritise to meet strict deadlines which will mean being flexible and responsive. The post holder will support the delivery of the portfolio of the research group, as directed by the Chief Investigator and Project Manager. Responsibilities will be varied and will include support with project management activities including data acquisition and management, ethics and regulatory approvals, quality control, reporting and document management in line with Research Governance standards. You will have experience of coordinating clinical trials within a clinical research environment, be enthusiastic and self-motivated, with good understanding of Good Clinical Practice and overarching regulatory guidelines. The post holder will be based within the Division of Cancer Sciences in the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health at St Mary’s Hospital. You will be responsible for: Assist the Project Manager as required in maintaining the Trial Master File and monitoring aspects of the project Work closely with the eight NHS cervical screening laboratories to ensure confidential and efficient transfer of linked clinical data Work closely with the research team to ensure adherence to the protocol and regulatory guidelines Discuss and monitor risks to delivery, implementing changes to the project plans as directed by the Project Manager, ensuring that specific timelines are met and delivering against the programme’s objectives Liaising with colleagues across University departments, other universities, and NHS organisations, facilitating effective communication and engagement with investigators and collaborators About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Good (2.2 or above) biological science or social science degree or equivalent research experience Supporting and managing research projects or clinical trials preferably in an academic and/or NHS setting Study coordination of clinical trials including all aspects of research governance and delivery of research whilst working to set deadlines Excellent organisation, managing a varied workload Excellent oral and written communication skills, demonstrating ability to communicate effectively at all levels in a multi-disciplinary environment Desirable Criteria Experience of the University of Manchester’s electronic ordering system (iProc) or a similar system Trained in GCP and with a good understanding of the Research Governance Framework and ethical guidelines for work with humans and/ or human tissue Experience of organising and minuting meetings Producing progress reports to funding bodies, research ethics committees, senior managers etc. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Chinenye Amadi Email Address: Chinenye.amadi@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032261 Research Project Coordinator Further Particulars (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45892": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032237 Salary: £37,694  - £38,784 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Immunology, Immunity to Infection, and Respiratory Medicine Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/09/2026 Contract Duration: 17 Months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Job Description We are recruiting a postdoctoral researcher to take up a position within the Swamy group in the University of Manchester, to study potential new therapeutic targets for treating Coeliac disease. This is a fixed-term appointment for 1 year 7 months starting in September 2026. This is an exciting opportunity to be at the interface of clinical and basic research, and to collaborate with Dr. Hugo Penny and Prof. David Sanders at the National Centre for Refractory Coeliac Disease in Sheffield. The project will primarily involve studying human intestinal lymphocytes obtained from patient samples, and investigating the effects of different immunomodulators in controlling or exacerbating disease states. Patient samples will be obtained and processed in Sheffield, therefore the successful candidate must be willing to travel to and from Sheffield. The candidate will have experience in, or receive training in cutting-edge proteomics methods, and will have experience in analysing big data. A keen interest in deciphering the signalling pathways that allow autoimmune activation of T cells in Coeliac disease is a must. The successful candidate will have the chance to be involved in ground-breaking research that has high translational potential. There will be opportunities to present the research in national and international meetings and to publish the research findings. In addition, public engagement and patient involvement will form a key part of this position. There will also be opportunities to develop the research further with industrial partners, clinicians and other researchers. The successful candidate will be trained in cutting-edge proteomic technologies, human immunology, -omics analyses, spectral flow cytometry, and other state-of-the-art techniques. This is a unique opportunity to identify disease-relevant mechanisms using primary patient samples, and cutting-edge techniques, in a world-class research-intensive setting. Your priorities will include: Strong interest in studying disease mechanisms of Coeliac disease Must be willing to travel to Sheffield monthly Confidence in project implementation, decision-making, coordinating collaborative research Must be willing to work in a team and assist with training of PhD students and other lab members Keen interest in presenting scientific work at both national and international meetings, as well as to the public, and to talk to patients We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. Who we’re looking for: The successful candidate will: Have a PhD in Immunology, clinical research, or similar with strong academic record, and at least one publication/preprint demonstrating research output Have a strong interest in understanding the molecular basis of Coeliac disease Have a strong background in immunology, inflammatory bowel diseases, and mass spectrometry Have established skills with primary cell cultures and proteomics or other -omics analyses Have a strong ability to work independently but with the communication skills and openness to work in a team, and the willingness to foster an open and collaborative approach to science Have excellent communication skills and knowledge of the English language We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Mahima Swamy Email Address: mahima.swamy@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032237 Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease) (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45773": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology and Genomics)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology and Genomics) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology and Genomics) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032123 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Institute of Biotechnology Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: 6 months initially (exact contract length dependent on start date) School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This position forms part of Manchester’s development of synthetic biology approaches for next generation biotechnology, and will be based in Professor Patrick Cai’s ( yizhi.cai@manchester.ac.uk ) research group. The group is located in the Manchester Institute of Biotechnology (MIB), a unique cross-disciplinary research institute at the University of Manchester, which brings together more than 450 researchers with expertise in the foundational sciences supporting Synthetic Biology, including molecular biology, chemistry, engineering, materials science, computer science and medicine and as such is at the forefront of international developments in the emerging science of Synthetic Biology. The Cai laboratory has pioneered the design, construction and characterization of completely synthetic eukaryotic chromosomes. The group is actively exploring new applications of artificial chromosome technology, including the generation of novel natural product-like chemical matter. The Cai lab was recently awarded an ARIA grant to develop synthetic plant artificial chromosomes, and a Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation Global Challenge pilot grant to develop artificial chromosome-based strategies for discovery of novel anti-TB agents. We are now seeking to recruit a skilled Senior Technician to join the team with a good understanding and practical experience of modern molecular biology and biochemistry techniques. The purpose of this role is to: Provide expertise in lab media preparation, particularly specialised yeast media (e.g. SC, YPD, etc), and perform cell culture for bacteria, yeast and plant cells as required. Support and assist our ongoing research projects where required, in the area of synthetic biology and genomics, under the supervision of the PI. Work with the PI to ensure full compliance in microbiological health and safety procedures and be responsible for training / oversight of correct microbiological techniques. Assist the PI in scheduling management / coordination, as well as organising research group activities, including lab meetings and other events, lab inductions, and ensure safety systems and procedures are kept up to date and relevant. Oversee daily laboratory operations and manage the co-ordination of a growing and diverse research group to keep the research environment functional, compliant and well organised. Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Derren Heyes Email: Derren.heyes@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032123 Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology & Genomics) FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45407": {
      "title": "Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health (Teaching & Research)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health (Teaching &amp; Research) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health (Teaching & Research) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031774 Salary: £75,914 - £82,933 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Jean McFarlane Building, University of Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Division of Nursing, Midwifery & Social Work Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/09/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to research, innovation and teaching and provide key leadership within the University and its partners, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will contribute to research, innovation and teaching across the organisation and provide key methodological leadership within the University. We are particularly seeking candidates who can demonstrate a strong research profile in community nursing and/or public health, in a relevant practice setting. Demonstrable impacts across digital healthcare, healthy aging, mental health, social research, long-term conditions or applied research methodologies, are particularly welcomed. You will be responsible for: Leading and contributing to the development of high-importance or interdisciplinary community nursing/public health research to achieve tangible benefits and impact for the School of Health Sciences Leading and sustaining research grant income and conduct research of national and international excellence funded by relevant external agencies Producing and disseminating high-quality, high-impact research outputs of national and international standing Providing cross-divisional PGR supervision and mentorship Providing academic leadership in your field, including the development, mentoring and career management of early and mid-career researchers, professional and research active colleagues We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A PhD in a relevant area Knowledge of community health nursing and/or public health nursing, research priorities and policy agendas A sustained record of success in obtaining research funding and delivering large-scale programmes and projects Evidence of leadership of research collaborations with national/international partners to advance global health or methodologies in the field A sustained record of successful doctoral supervision and assessments and PGR/PGT examinations Desirable Criteria: Current registration with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC) or relevant professional organisation Specialist Qualifications in Community Nursing/Public Health Nursing We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Dawn Dowding, Head of Division Email Address: dawn.dowding@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Interviews for this post are expected to take place between 26th October and 6th November 2026. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-031774 Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health Further Particulars (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45893": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032245 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to research into the electrical and optical characterisation of defects in high aluminium content AlGaN with a view to increasing the efficiency of UVC light emitting diodes within a dynamic and inclusive environment. The successful applicant will use advanced characterisation techniques to investigate defect behaviour and contribute to high-quality research outputs and international collaborations. You will be responsible for: - The electrical and optical characterisation of defects in high aluminium content AlGaN with a view to increasing the efficiency of UVC light emitting diodes. - Use Deep Level Transient Spectroscopy (DLTS) and photoluminescence measurements to characterise the impact of these defects on device performance. - Analyse the data produced by these experiments and contribute to the writing of journal papers based on the results. - Present work at academic conferences, both within the UK and overseas. - Travel to the Tyndall institute in Cork, Ireland, for collaboration meetings. - We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Please see the Further Particulars document linked below for the full person specification criteria. Desirable Criteria: Please see the Further Particulars document linked below for the full person specification criteria. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer contribution pension - 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure - Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof Matthew Halsall Email Address: matthew.halsall@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Support Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032245 - Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45895": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Health Sciences Research",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Health Sciences Research Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Health Sciences Research Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032239 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Dentistry Hours Per Week: 17.5 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term until 31/05/2029 School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Health Sciences Research . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to applied health research activities across the Division of Dentistry within a dynamic and inclusive environment. The Division is home to Cochrane Oral Health, and a central aspect of the role will be supporting the delivery of high-quality systematic reviews that inform clinical practice, guideline development and health policy, helping to advance evidence-based oral health care. You will be responsible for: Strengthen evidence synthesis capacity within the Division through the conduct of high-quality systematic reviews. Support research impact, dissemination and stakeholder engagement activities, including through websites and social media. Support the development and submission of external funding applications. Contribute to teaching and assessment activities, as appropriate. Develop an independent academic trajectory, including applications for doctoral or postdoctoral fellowships. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Hold, or be nearing completion of, a PhD in a subject relevant to applied health sciences or a related discipline, or possess equivalent research experience. Experience of quantitative research and research methodology. Experience of conducting Cochrane and/or non-Cochrane systematic reviews. Experience of critical appraisal and meta-analysis. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with clinical and non-clinical collaborators. Desirable Criteria: Experience of network meta-analysis and/or meta-regression. Experience of stakeholder or public involvement in research. Experience of authoring peer-reviewed publications. Willingness to travel to national and international meetings. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Anne-Marie Glenny Email Address: warren.mittoo@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Support Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032239 - Research Associate in Health Sciences Research - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45510": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing Apply for job Job reference: HUM-031915 Salary: £42,254 - £71,566 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Management Sciences and Marketing Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/09/2026 Contract Duration: permanent School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a T&R Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to actively to the research environment of the Marketing Group, the Division and the School, as well as contributing to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching across the programmes offered by the Division, in Manchester, online and, where appropriate, at our Global Centres, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. In your application, please indicate clearly whether you are applying for a Lecturer or a Senior Lecturer role. We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Emma Banister Email Address: Emma.Banister@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt HUM-031915 Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing FPs1 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45810": {
      "title": "Divisional Administrator(Redeployees Only)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Divisional Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Divisional Administrator (Redeployees Only) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032159 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Pharmacy and Optometry Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated, organised and collaborative individual to join our Division of Pharmacy and Optometry as a Divisional Administrator. This role provides comprehensive and proactive PA, administrative, operational, people services and finance support to staff and activities across the Division. You will help ensure the smooth running of day-to-day divisional activity by coordinating meetings, events, recruitment and administrative processes, while working professionally with colleagues and stakeholders across the University. Please note that the earliest start date for this post is Monday 2nd November 2026 You will be responsible for: Providing proactive PA and administrative support, including diary coordination, meeting arrangements, agenda preparation, minute taking and following up agreed actions. Coordinating divisional meetings, committees, seminars, visitor programmes and events, ensuring logistics, materials and related arrangements are in place. Supporting recruitment and people-related administration, including selection arrangements, interview coordination, appointments, onboarding and induction activity. Supporting finance and procurement administration, including purchasing, expenses, invoice-related activity, payment forms and liaison with finance colleagues. Maintaining effective communication with academic, research, professional services and external stakeholders, signposting queries appropriately and escalating matters where required. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate the following essential criteria: Essential Criteria Experience of working in an administrative, customer-facing role and providing effective PA support, including diary management and meeting coordination. Experience of supporting meetings, committees or events, including arranging logistics, preparing documentation, taking minutes and following up actions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work professionally with a wide range of colleagues and stakeholders. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, meet deadlines, maintain accuracy and resolve routine problems effectively. Good IT skills, particularly in Microsoft 365 applications, with a proactive, flexible and collaborative approach to supporting colleagues and responding to changing priorities. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Paula Lee Email Address: paula.lee@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt Divisional Administrator - G4 - JD BMH-032159 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/web-accessibility/": {
      "title": "Web accessibility statement",
      "description": "Web accessibility | The University of Manchester Skip to navigation | Skip to main content | Skip to footer Home Study Undergraduate Results and Confirmation Undergraduate Courses Prospectus (undergraduate) Applying (undergraduate) Offer-holders Fees and funding (undergraduate) Widening participation programmes Study experience (undergraduate) Student support (undergraduate) Contact us (undergraduate) Taught master's Taught master's Courses Admissions (taught master's) Fees and funding for taught master's study Why should I study a master’s? Teaching and learning (taught master's) Meet us (taught master's) Student support (taught master's) Why Manchester? (taught master's) Contact us (taught master's) Postgraduate research Why Manchester? (postgraduate research) Research areas Postgraduate research Programmes People (postgraduate research) Admissions (postgraduate research) Fees for postgraduate research Funding for postgraduate research Contact us (postgraduate research) Postgraduate certificates and diplomas Online and blended learning Continuing professional development courses Student terms and conditions, policies and procedures Student life Campus spaces Student experience City guide Open days Campus tours Accommodation Get ready for Manchester Virtual Manchester Making a payment International Why study at Manchester? (for international students) Accommodation for international students English education system International admissions Finance and scholarships for international students Study abroad and exchange Country-specific information Exhibitions and visits in your country Contact the international team Research Research highlights Advanced materials – research highlights Biotechnology – research highlights Cancer – research highlights Energy – research highlights Global inequalities – research highlights Research structure Research institutes Research platforms Research facilities Our Faculties Research strategy and achievements Research Explorer Researchers Publications Projects Research impact and innovation REF 2021 (research) Research impact showcase (research) SDG campaign (research) Research partnerships Postgraduate research Bicentenary PhD Studentships and Fellowships Researcher development Research environment Research vacancies Collaborate Business engagement and knowledge exchange Innovation Factory Policy Innovation Festival Make a donation Inspiring local communities Global influence About Our story Strategy to 2035 University structure People Key dates Rankings Our campus Maps and travel Open to the public (visitor attractions) Facilities Conference venues Our bicentenary year History and heritage Social responsibility Teaching and learning excellence Environmental sustainability News Events The University Magazine Governance Privacy information Connect Jobs Alumni Teachers and careers advisers Social media Contact us Give Close menu Share via Share by Email Share via Facebook Share via Facebook Messenger Share via Twitter Share via LinkedIn Share via Pinterest Share via Reddit Search Search text Alternatively, use our A–Z index Close menu Home Connect Jobs Search jobs Benefits of working here Disabled applicants International applicants Equality, diversity and inclusion Inclusive career opportunities Relocating to Manchester Application hints and tips Flexible working Home Connect Jobs Web accessibility Share Home Connect Jobs Search jobs Benefits of working here Disabled applicants International applicants Equality, diversity and inclusion Inclusive career opportunities Relocating to Manchester Application hints and tips Flexible working Web accessibility statement www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk The University of Manchester is committed to making its websites accessible, in accordance with the Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018. This accessibility statement applies to The University of Manchester Jobs Website at www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk. Compliance status This website is partially compliant with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines version 2.2 AA standard, due to ‘the non-compliances’, ‘the exemptions’ or ‘the non-compliances and exemptions’ listed below. Non-accessible content The content listed below is non-accessible for the following reason(s): Non-compliance with the accessibility regulations WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard Not all functionality within the content is operable through a keyboard interface. Users will face accessibility barriers as keyboard-only navigation is unavailable, limiting access for individuals with motor disabilities who rely on keyboard input. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.1.1 (Keyboard). WCAG 2.4.7 Focus Visible Without a visible keyboard focus indicator, users who rely on keyboard navigation may struggle to know where they are on the page, leading to confusion and making the website difficult to use, especially for those with disabilities. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.4.7 (Focus Visible). WCAG 1.4.10 Reflow Content may require excessive scrolling. Users on small screens may struggle to access content, leading to frustration and lost information. Poor layout design can obscure important content, making the website difficult to navigate and diminishing the overall user experience. This fails WCAG success criterion 1.4.10 (Reflow). WCAG 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) When interactive elements like buttons or links are too small, users with motor impairments, such as those with tremors, arthritis, or limited dexterity, may find it difficult to accurately tap or click on them. This lack of precision can lead to mistakes, such as unintended clicks, which can frustrate users and hinder their ability to navigate the site efficiently. On touch devices, especially with smaller screens, the problem is magnified, making it harder for users to access important features or content, potentially excluding them from fully using the site. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name When the visible label of an interface element doesn’t match its programmatic label, users relying on assistive technologies may experience confusion. This inconsistency can make it harder to navigate and interact with the site, leading to frustration and preventing users from completing tasks or accessing important content. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.5.3 Label in Name. WCAG 1.4.3 Contrast (minimum) The visual presentation of text and images of text does not have a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1. Users may have trouble perceiving, understanding and interacting with content due to the poor contrast of content and navigation. This fails WCAG success criterion 1.4.3 (Contrast (Minimum)) WCAG 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context) The purpose of every link on the webpage isn't clear by reading the link text or by considering the text alongside its surrounding context. This means users don't know exactly where the link will take them or what it will do when clicked, creating confusion and frustration when unintended actions occur. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.4.4 Link purpose. WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value For all user interface components, the name and role cannot be programmatically determined; states, properties, and values that cannot be set by the user or programmatically set; and notification of changes to these items is not available to user agents, including assistive technologies. Users will have trouble understanding and interacting with elements as they lack proper labelling, role assignment, or value identification, leading to confusion and reduced accessibility, especially for users relying on assistive technologies. This fails WCAG success criterion 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value). We are working with our supplier to remediate the above non-compliances and expect these to be complete Q4, 2024. Content is not within the scope of the accessibility regulations PDFs and other documents The accessibility regulations do not require us to fix PDFs or other documents published before 23 September 2018 if they’re not essential to providing our services. Any new PDFs or Word documents we publish will meet accessibility standards. Live video We do not plan to add captions to live video streams because live video is exempt from meeting the accessibility regulations What we’re doing to improve accessibility The University of Manchester is committed to improving digital inclusion and equity, ensuring that everyone has equitable access to information, technology, resources, and opportunities for learning and engagement, regardless of ability. We are undertaking a program of activity working with our Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion Division and Disability Advisory and Support Service to improve digital experiences for users with disabilities. Preparation of this accessibility statement This statement was prepared on 22/10/2024. It was last reviewed on 23/10/2024. This website was last tested on 14 October 2024. The test was carried out internally by The University of Manchester. The most relevant pages were tested using automated testing tools by The IT Services Division. Pages tested: Current jobs list Candidate login An individual job listing Apply for a job > I don't work here Feedback and contact information You can report accessibility issues and request support you encounter with this website using the following contact information. We aim to respond to your request in 3-5 days. Email: accessibility@manchester.ac.uk Enforcement procedure The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC) is responsible for enforcing the Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018 (the ‘accessibility regulations’). If you’re not happy with how we respond to your complaint, contact the Equality Advisory and Support Service (EASS) . Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Contact details Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Road Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer Data Protection Copyright notice Web accessibility Freedom of information Charitable status Royal Charter Number: RC000797 Privacy preference centre Your privacy Strictly Necessary Cookies Performance Cookies Functional Cookies Targeting Cookies Your privacy When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. This information might be about you, your preferences, or your device, and is mostly used to make the site work as you expect. The information does not usually identify you directly, but it can give you a more personalized web experience. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. Click on the different category headings to learn more and change our default settings. Blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. More information Strictly Necessary Cookies Always active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. These cookies do not store any personally identifiable information. Performance Cookies Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources, so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. All information these cookies collect is aggregated and therefore anonymous. If you do not allow these cookies, we will not know when you have visited our site. Functional Cookies Functional Cookies These cookies enable the website to provide enhanced functionality and personalisation. They may be set by us or by third-party providers whose services we have added to our pages. If you do not allow these cookies then some or all of these services may not function properly. Targeting Cookies Targeting Cookies These cookies may be set through our site by our advertising partners. They may be used by those companies to build a profile of your interests and show you relevant adverts on other sites. They do not store directly personal information, but are based on uniquely identifying your browser and internet device. If you do not allow these cookies, you will experience less targeted advertising. Back Button Back Filter Button Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Confirm my choices Allow all"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45787": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation and Inspection",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation and Inspection Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation and Inspection Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032140 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/09/2026 Contract Duration: 36 Months 1st October 2026 or as soon as possible to 30th September 2029 with a review after 2 years School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Purpose of the Job The FENDER programme aims to bring game-changing ideas and exploit advances in complimentary areas of science to trigger a UK-led renaissance in electromagnetic NDE, boosting the UK’s reputation, energising a world-leading research community and achieving downstream commercial impact via a broad network of industrial partners. The overarching ambition is to create a world-leading, agile, strongly connected research programme that addresses the foundation science topics essential for innovation to produce the next generation of electromagnetic (EM) non-destructive evaluation tools and techniques. A further aspiration is to pave the way in deploying these advances with our industry partners across the engineering sector. The Programme is led by the University of Manchester and involves four other Universities (Bristol, Warwick, Newcastle and Strathclyde). The initiative is supported by approximately 25 industrial partners which are some of the major NDE users. The vision for FENDER is to catapult advanced EM sensing technologies to the forefront of industrial monitoring and manufacturing processes, maximising quality assurance and control capabilities, and firmly establishing these powerful enabling technologies at the core of future industrial processes. Harnessing the combined knowledge and capabilities of the academic and industrial partners’ expertise in this field, we will create a unique platform for accelerating physics-led EM sensing technologies into impact in NDE applications. FENDER will establish the UK as a global powerhouse for EM sensing technologies, building the foundation for innovative collaborative research, commercial enterprise and economic impact, providing a launchpad for industry and innovators to facilitate Industry 4.0 and beyond. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Tony Peyton Email: a.peyton@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46044": {
      "title": "Clinical Lecturer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Clinical Lecturer Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Clinical Lecturer Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032385 Salary: Clinical ACT5 (£48,309 - £82,884) Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Division of Psychology and Mental Health Hours Per Week: 17.5 hours per week (0.5 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 25/09/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Clinical Lecturer (Research and Teaching focused) within the Division of Psychology and Mental Health, School of Health Sciences, Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. This permanent clinical academic lecturer position offers an exciting opportunity to pursue collaborative research in an area relevant to clinical psychology and to engage in knowledge transfer activities aimed at maximising the impact of your work. You will also provide clinically relevant teaching, research supervision and academic support on the Clinical Psychology Doctorate (ClinPsyD). You will be responsible for: Carry out research at nationally and internationally recognised levels of excellence that is aligned with the priorities of the Division and Faculty, and to regularly publish the results in leading outlets Apply for and win research funds from external sources at a level which allows them to be competitive in their field and to create opportunities for next generation researchers Undertake ClinPsyD and PhD supervision in a manner that satisfies the Manchester Doctoral College’s expectations for the supervision of research students Design and deliver inclusive and effective lectures, workshops and seminars for students on the ClinPsyD Provide appropriate academic and pastoral support and guidance to students on the ClinPsyD We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A Clinical Psychology Doctorate OR a Statement of Equivalence stating that the holder’s qualification in clinical psychology is recognised within the UK Professional registration as a Practitioner Psychologist with the Health and Care Professions Council [N.B. the post-holders will be responsible for maintaining this registration/accreditation throughout their tenure] A growing record of relevant research as evidenced by significant publications in peer-reviewed outlets, some external funding and a clear trajectory to important impacts within and beyond academia Emerging evidence of research independence and the ability to lead projects Experience of successful teaching for undergraduate and/or postgraduate students Desirable Criteria A PhD in psychology, clinical psychology or a related academic discipline A teaching qualification or fellowship appropriate to higher education (e.g. HEA / Advance HE) Experience of successful completion of service or leadership roles in academia or the NHS We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Peter Taylor, Clinical Reader and Programme Director, Clinical Psychology Doctorate (ClinPsyD) Email Address: peter.taylor-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars with person specification linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45956": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032344 Salary: £29,588 - £31,236 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 17/09/2026 Contract Duration: 24 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Technician to provide technical support for a research programme investigating vascular mechanisms relevant to Alzheimer's disease and other neurodegenerative conditions. The role will support laboratory-based studies focused on amyloid beta, protein interactions and the development of potential therapeutic approaches. This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to a collaborative academic-industry research programme, gaining hands-on experience in protein and amyloid research. Working closely with researchers and technical colleagues, you will support routine laboratory activities, generate high-quality experimental data and contribute to the effective operation of a research laboratory within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Providing laboratory support for research activities including protein and amyloid preparation, buffer preparation and related experimental procedures. Supporting protein-protein interaction studies, including surface plasmon resonance (SPR) experiments and other relevant assays. Contributing to the analysis, organisation and presentation of experimental data for reports, publications and presentations. Supporting the safe and efficient operation of laboratory facilities, including stock management, procurement and laboratory housekeeping. Assisting with laboratory inductions, equipment use, health and safety compliance, and the preparation of risk assessments and standard operating procedures. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A degree in Biochemistry, Cell Biology or a related biological science subject, or equivalent qualifications and substantial laboratory experience. Practical laboratory experience in biochemistry, cell biology or a related biological science discipline. Experience of cell culture, protein-protein interaction assays or related molecular biology techniques. Strong organisational skills with the ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively both independently and as part of a team. Excellent communication, analytical and problem-solving skills, with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Desirable Criteria Experience of surface plasmon resonance (SPR) or other protein-binding techniques. Experience preparing standard operating procedures and laboratory risk assessments. Experience using scientific and analytical software packages. Willingness to support training and induction activities within the laboratory. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Adam Greenstein Email: adam.greenstein@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45958": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Catalysis and Machine Learning",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research Associate in Catalysis and Machine Learning Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research Associate in Catalysis and Machine Learning Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032299 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum dependent on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemistry Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/10/2026 Contract Duration: 24 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description A Postdoctoral Research Associate position is available for outstanding and ambitious researchers to join Prof. Igor Larrosa’s team at the University of Manchester, working on Catalysis, Organometallic, Organic Chemistry and Machine Learning for a period of up to 24 months. The project, funded by EPSRC, will involve exploring the use of machine learning to develop new tools for investigating catalytic reaction mechanisms, with a focus on homogeneous catalytic processes of interest in organic chemistry. This collaborative project with the Bures group (ICIQ) will build on proof-of-principle work published in Nature 2023, 613, 689. Overall Purpose of the Job Find out more about the Larrosa and Bures groups at: https://personalpages.manchester.ac.uk/staff/igor.larrosa/home.html https://jordibures.wixsite.com/buresgroup The successful candidate will have a PhD in chemistry, with a proven track record in catalysis, organic and organometallic chemistry, and mechanistic investigation. The position is full time and available for up to two years, in the first instance. Informal enquiries can be made to Prof. Igor Larrosa ( igor.larrosa@manchester.ac.uk ) Applications should include a supporting statement and a CV, as part of the application. Forms and further particulars are available from our website www.manchester.ac.uk/jobs The Department of Chemistry is strongly committed to promoting equality and diversity, including the Athena SWAN Charter for gender equality in higher education. The School holds a Silver Award which recognises their good practice in relation to gender; including flexible working arrangements, family-friendly policies, and support to allow staff to achieve a good work-life balance. We particularly welcome applications from women for this post. Appointment will always be made on merit. For further information, please visit http://www.chemistry.manchester.ac.uk/about-us/athena-swan/ Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Blended working arrangements may be considered Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Igor Larrosa Email: igor.larrosa@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Email: https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46004": {
      "title": "The University of Manchester",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for JUST CENTRE ADMINISTRATOR Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results JUST CENTRE ADMINISTRATOR Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032346 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: SoSS Research Centres Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 24/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term until 2nd February 2030 School/Directorate: School of Social Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a JUST Research Centre Administrator. This role offers an exciting opportunity to provide comprehensive and proactive administrative and operational support for the delivery of the JUST Research Centre and associated activities within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Maintain good working relationships within the Centre and with the respective stakeholders both internal and external by offering excellent customer care service: relaying email messages, prompt answering or escalating of queries, welcoming and registering guests. Assisting with the preparation of paperwork relating to grant applications and reports to funders. Maintain accurate mailing lists for academics, researchers and external stakeholders and circulate the relevant information within the research group. Support colleagues by proactively finding solutions for problems presented and work independently to find resolution, recognising when escalation is required. Support the organisation and delivery of grant related research activities (quotations and bookings for venues, booking travel and accommodation, arranging catering, monitoring registrations etc) as necessary. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Administrative experience within a busy office environment Demonstrate organisational skills and attention to detail Good verbal and written communication skills demonstrating an ability to deal with a diverse range of individuals both internal and external to the organisation Demonstrate team-working skills to work within a collaborative environment and liaise with academics, administrators and wider University and external teams Ability to work with minimum supervision and to show initiative and ability to adapt to constantly changing challenges Desirable Criteria Experience of software packages including Eventbrite, Asana, Canva Knowledge and experience of using University systems such as Oracle, iProc, PURE and Key Travel Experience of research administration in an HE environment Experience of working in a process driven team environment, demonstrating an ability to provide support for colleagues Interest in issues related to the areas of research of the Centre: environmental sustainability, decarbonisation/net zero, and social justice We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Doyin Mansell Email Address: doyin.mansell@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt HUM-032346 - JUST CENTRE ADMINISTRATOR (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45769": {
      "title": "Media Production Technician (Part-Time 0.5 FTE)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Media Production Technician (Part-Time 0.5 FTE) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Media Production Technician (Part-Time 0.5 FTE) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032273 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 pro rata, per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Campus Technology Services Hours Per Week: 17.5 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 24/09/2026 Contract Duration: permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of IT Services Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description About the role The University of Manchester is one of the UK’s largest single-campus universities and offers a dynamic, rewarding environment in which to work and develop. Media Services, within Campus Technology Services, provides professional media production, audio-visual support, live event technical delivery, graphic design and specialist print services across the University. We are seeking an enthusiastic and creative Media Production Technician to join our Media Production team on a part-time basis. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to the production of high-quality audio and visual media for teaching, learning, research, communications and high-profile University events. What you will do Capture high-quality audio and video content in studios, production facilities, campus spaces and external locations. Edit and post-produce media content, including colour grading, audio mastering, motion graphics and animation. Support the technical delivery of live events, including camera operation, vision mixing and streaming. Produce graphics and animations for promotional, teaching and learning materials. Support staff and students using Media Services’ editing, podcast and production facilities. Develop and deliver training workshops and supporting materials for individuals and groups. Maintain awareness of emerging media production tools, workflows and technologies. Work safely and professionally, contributing to a customer-focused and continuously improving service About you You will bring good knowledge of professional audio and visual media production, with practical experience in production and post-production workflows. You will be confident using cameras, lighting, audio capture, editing and mastering tools, and you will have the creativity to produce engaging, professional content for a wide range of audiences. You should have an HND equivalent qualification in media production or a related discipline, or demonstrable professional skills and knowledge gained through relevant industry experience. Strong communication skills, excellent interpersonal skills, a high level of IT literacy and the ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team are essential. Experience of live production, location work, motion graphics, immersive or 360° media production, and a showreel of previous work would be advantageous although not essential. What you will get in return A collaborative and supportive working environment within a busy professional media services team. The opportunity to contribute directly to teaching, learning, research, events and University-wide activity. Access to learning and development opportunities. A generous pension scheme and employee health and wellbeing support. Annual leave entitlement, bank holidays and additional paid closure over the Christmas period, subject to University terms and conditions. Access to a range of local and national staff discounts and benefits. Further information As an equal opportunities employer, The University of Manchester welcomes applicants from all sections of the community. Appointments are made on merit. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Trevor Byrne Email: trevor.byrne@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support JobTrainSupport This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45918": {
      "title": "The University of Manchester",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for CRADLE Project Manager (Maternity Cover) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results CRADLE Project Manager (Maternity Cover) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032263 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: School of Engineering Administration Hours Per Week: 1 FTE 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/09/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The University of Manchester has recently established the Manchester Centre for Robotics and Artificial Intelligence and is a recognised lead in the development of robots for extreme environments, particularly nuclear. The CRADLE Project Manager will be located in Manchester to support the recently awarded EPSRC Prosperity Partnership: Centre for Robotic Autonomy in Demanding and Long-lasting Environments (CRADLE). CRADLE is a collaborative programme with Amentum involving approximately 20 researchers from The University of Manchester and a similar number of engineers from Amentum. The primary aim of CRADLE is to develop novel technologies that will allow autonomous robots to be reliably deployed in demanding environments, such as space, nuclear and urban infrastructure, for long lengths of time. The CRADLE Project Manager will be responsible for providing project management services and support to the ongoing research activities within the CRADLE programme as well as the wider robotics group in Manchester. The postholder will be a key point of contact for stakeholder engagement, in particular with external stakeholders where regular engagement is expected including the facilitation of technology demonstrations and a professional approach to working is required. It is expected that the postholder will also support the facilitation of research and technical demonstrations that are conducted at Amentum’s facility in Birchwood. It is expected that the postholder will contribute to the writing of high-quality publications on experimental activities and take an active role in the dissemination of research. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jenn Dunsford Email: jenn.dunsford@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032263 CRADLE Project Manager FP'S (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45987": {
      "title": "The University of Manchester",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Principal Architects Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Principal Architects Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032327 Salary: £59,966 - £71,566 + £24,421 Market Supplement Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Enterprise and Business Architecture Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/09/2026 Contract Duration: 24 School/Directorate: Directorate of IT Services Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Shape the future architecture of one of the UK's leading universities. The University of Manchester is seeking two Principal Architects to help shape and deliver the architectural direction of a major portfolio of change and transformation activity. This is an opportunity to influence enterprise-wide initiatives, contribute to long-term technology strategy and support the delivery of the University's ambitions through effective architecture planning, governance and collaboration. As a senior member of the Enterprise and Business Architecture team, you will work alongside executive sponsors, business leaders, technology teams and delivery partners to ensure strategic objectives are translated into clear architecture roadmaps and achievable change plans. You will contribute to investment discussions, help shape future demand and support informed decision-making across the University. What you'll be doing • Shaping architecture strategy, planning and roadmap development across a major University value stream. • Developing and maintaining architecture blueprints, future-state visions and transition plans that support delivery of the University's strategic goals. • Building effective partnerships with stakeholders and acting as a trusted adviser on architectural, technological and strategic matters. • Working with colleagues across programmes and projects to provide architectural guidance and ensure alignment with enterprise architecture principles, standards and roadmaps. • Identifying opportunities for simplification, reuse and continuous improvement while helping teams navigate risks, dependencies and complexity. • Supporting architecture governance and assurance activities, helping stakeholders understand and navigate key decision-making processes. • Sharing insight on emerging technologies and future opportunities that could enhance teaching, research and professional services. • Coaching, developing and supporting Business Service Architects and Business Analysts aligned to the portfolio. About you We're looking for someone who enjoys bringing people together to solve complex challenges and can balance long-term thinking with practical delivery. You'll be comfortable building relationships across a large organisation and helping colleagues make informed choices in a collaborative environment. You will bring: • Significant experience in enterprise architecture, architecture leadership, technology strategy or a related discipline within a large and complex organisation. • A strong understanding of business architecture, applications, data, integration, infrastructure and security considerations. • Experience working with senior stakeholders and providing clear, evidence-based recommendations to support strategic outcomes. • A track record of developing architecture roadmaps, target states and transformation plans. • Experience of architecture governance, assurance and design decision-making. • Excellent communication skills and the ability to explain complex concepts to both technical and non-technical audiences. • Experience supporting, developing and enabling high-performing teams. Professional certifications such as TOGAF, BCS Architecture, cloud architecture certifications or experience with architecture modelling frameworks such as ArchiMate would be advantageous. Why join Manchester? The University of Manchester is one of the world's leading universities, recognised for its pioneering research, outstanding teaching and global impact. You'll have the opportunity to work on complex, organisation-wide challenges, contributing to the technology landscape that supports our students, staff and researchers for years to come. We recognise that strong candidates may not meet every requirement listed. If you're excited about the opportunity and believe your skills and experience are relevant, we'd encourage you to apply. We welcome applications from people with a variety of career backgrounds and value collaboration, curiosity, continuous learning and different perspectives alongside technical expertise. Our benefits • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays and Christmas closure • Ride to Work and Electric Vehicle schemes • Access to a wide range of learning and development opportunities General enquiries about the role Lewis Burne Talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Supporting Documents save_alt Principal Architect Candidate Brief (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45930": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032271 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Pharmacy and Optometry Hours Per Week: Full time (1.0 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months (start date 15th of October or as soon as practical) School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a PhD, with a background in polymer science and drug delivery, to work within the Division of Pharmacy and Optometry under the supervision of Prof Alberto Saiani. The post holder will be part of the Polymer & Peptides Research Group, which is based at Manchester Institute of Biotechnology. The candidate will support the aims of the project funded by Industrial Biotechnology Innovation Catalyst (IBIC). This innovative project will study the design and development of novel in-situ forming sub-cutaneous solid depot for long term delivery of cancer drugs. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic, well-motivated individual to work as part of a sociable multi-disciplinary team consisting of PhD and PDRA. The ideal candidate will have excellent scientific and communication skills and will be able to work with minimal supervision leading the interaction with industrial collaborator Innovarus. What you will get in return: • Fantastic market leading Pension scheme • Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme • Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays • Additional paid closure over the Christmas period • Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk. Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note that an Academic Technology Approval Scheme (ATAS) certificate is required for this role. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Alberto Saiani Email: a.saiani@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain Client Support Hub This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45972": {
      "title": "The University of Manchester",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Data and Analytics Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Data and Analytics Administrator Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032314 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Hybrid Working on Campus/Remote Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: People Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/09/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: People Directorate Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Data and Analytics Administrator . This role offers an exciting opportunity to support the delivery of the University’s statutory, strategic and operational reporting requirements within a dynamic and inclusive People Services environment. Working with Power BI, Oracle reporting tools and other in-house systems, you will help analyse, interpret and present data-driven insights to business stakeholders. You will be responsible for: Develop, maintain and monitor a range of reports to support statutory, strategic and operational reporting requirements, including HESA, Office for Students (OfS) and Office for National Statistics (ONS) returns and related data requirements. Lead and facilitate stakeholder engagement to understand business requirements, translate them into effective reporting and analytics solutions, and identify opportunities for continuous improvement and better-informed decision making. Work collaboratively with stakeholders across the University to identify, investigate and resolve data quality issues, liaising with wider business teams and IT colleagues where appropriate. Support colleagues with processes and activities relating to the HESA Staff Return, ensuring data is accurate, complete and submitted within required deadlines. Act as a first point of contact for incoming reporting and data escalations, assessing and prioritising issues, troubleshooting root causes, resolving problems where possible and appropriately escalating or routing more complex issues. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Experience of using data analytics and reporting tools to manipulate, analyse and interpret data across systems and applications to produce business intelligence, Management Information and actionable insights. Experience of using Power BI and/or Oracle reporting tools, with the ability to develop and maintain reports that meet business and stakeholder requirements. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to engage effectively with a wide range of stakeholders with varying levels of data literacy and technical understanding. Excellent organisational and administrative skills, with a strong attention to detail and the ability to produce accurate, high-quality documentation and reporting outputs. Knowledge and practical understanding of data protection principles and their application within the workplace, including the appropriate handling of personal and sensitive information. Desirable Criteria: Experience of working within the Higher Education sector is desirable. Knowledge and understanding of operational business processes within Human Resources, Payroll and Pensions administration. Demonstrable commitment to continuous improvement, learning and professional development, with an ability to identify opportunities to improve processes, systems and services. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Email address: davine.cooke@manchester.ac.uk Name: Davine Cooke General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt UoM Job Description Data & Analytics Administrator 2026  (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45966": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032307 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Pharmacy and Optometry Hours Per Week: Full time (1.0 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 6 months (start date 15th of October or as soon as practical) School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Job Description *Duration is 6 months (start date 15th of October or as soon as practical) This is an outstanding opportunity for a PhD, with a background in material characterisation, to work within the Manchester Institute of Biotechnology (MIB) under the supervision of Prof Alberto Saiani. The post holder will be part of the Polymer and Peptide Research Group (www.polymersandpeptides.co.uk). The candidate will support the aims of the project funded by Industrial Biotechnology Innovation Catalyst (IBIC). This innovative project will study the fabric surfaces binding mechanisms of novel multi-functional proteins engineered to deliver diverse consumer benefits. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic, well-motivated individual to work as part of a sociable multi-disciplinary team consisting of PhD and PDRA. The ideal candidate will have excellent scientific and communication skills and will be able to work with minimal supervision leading the interaction with industrial collaborator Unilever. Experience of material characterisation techniques: QCM, AFM, ToF-SIMS and/or TEM is essential for the position. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note that an Academic Technology Approval Scheme (ATAS) certificate is required for this role. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Prof Alberto Saiani Email: a.saiani@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain Client Support Hub This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=35043": {
      "title": "Marketing Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Marketing Administrative Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Marketing Administrative Assistant Apply for job Job reference: HUM-031639 Salary: 23,742 - 25,249 depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Humanities Marketing and Communications Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/09/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Humanities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Student Marketing and Recruitment Assistant. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to student marketing and recruitment activity across the Faculty of Humanities, supporting the creation and delivery of campaigns that engage prospective students. Working as part of a dynamic and inclusive team, you will develop professional knowledge, skills and experience within higher education administration. You will be responsible for: - To work under the direction of SMR colleagues (Student Recruitment and Marketing Co-ordinators, Officers or Managers) to support the delivery of Student Marketing and Recruitment projects and campaigns. - To support clerical and administration activities following processes and procedures, as directed by the line manager. This will include tasks such as processing student ambassador timesheets. - Provide support with CRM activity with prospective students. This support will be administrative (uploading contacts, cleaning and de-duping data following agreed processes), and populating email and message templates with content. - Support the production and distribution of communications for prospective students and staff audiences. This will include proof reading colleagues’ work, and checking copy against house-style guidelines. - Undertake defined tasks that contribute to supporting the organisation, set-up, delivery and take-down of on-campus events such as open days and school/college visits. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria - Good communication skills, including copywriting and proofreading experience. - Good interpersonal skills. - Knowledge and understanding of all major social media channels. - Good organisational skills, and able to prioritise work. - Demonstrate an ability to work in a team environment and support colleagues. Desirable Criteria - Experience of communicating with a student or youth-orientated audience. - A keen interest in photography and/or video production and/or experience of basic design software. - An interest in e-marketing and some technical awareness e.g. basic HTML. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer pension contribution - Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays - Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements online. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Lauryn Edwards lauryn.edwards@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Candidate Support This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45575": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Civil Engineering Hydrodynamics",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer in Civil Engineering Hydrodynamics Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer in Civil Engineering Hydrodynamics Apply for job Job reference: SAE-031937 Salary: £47,389 to £58,225 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Department of Civil Engineering and Management Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 29/09/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking excellent candidates who aspire to become global leaders in Civil Engineering Hydrodynamics, with a passion to make substantial contribution to exciting delivery and syllabus development at undergraduate and postgraduate level. In particular we invite applications from candidates with substantial experience in one or more of the following areas of research: Development of fluids-based strategies tailored to sustainable & resilient infrastructure (including nature-based solutions, soft engineering, hydraulic structures), clean energy, complex fluids including sediments, pollutants, microplastics; by means of numerical and experimental approaches including high-performance computing, data science methods via machine learning/AI and digital twins, enhancement, development and of application advanced numerical and experimental methods for simulating fluids and multi-physics including mesh-based methods, spectral methods, and particle-based methods applied to multiphase flows and civil engineering applications. The role holder will develop research and teaching in research areas relating to civil engineering hydrodynamics focusing on numerical simulation, experimental and field investigations and disruptive technologies. This will entail collaboration with colleagues within the School and across the wider University, as well as more broadly with industrial and international collaborators and stakeholders, to develop a portfolio of cutting-edge disciplinary or interdisciplinary research activity. The appointee will lead on the delivery and development of teaching on fundamental topics and topics related to hydrodynamics and computational engineering for either disciplinary or interdisciplinary programmes in the School with opportunities to also contribute to cross University programmes. The Department of Civil Engineering and Management has world-renowned research including offshore renewable energy and environmental engineering, engineering management, geo-environmental engineering, structural safety and resilience, and the Tyndall Centre. Within the field of civil engineering hydrodynamics, we have world-leading research groups in offshore engineering and renewable energy, sustainable and resilient infrastructure and natural hazards. With the new Manchester Hydrodynamics Laboratory and high-fidelity numerical modelling including smoothed particle hydrodynamics (SPH) and turbulence modelling, the University is one of the leading institutions in the UK for innovative and impactful research to address the most pressing issues currently facing society. Faculty members are encouraged to participate in collaborative, multidisciplinary research that spans across the University’s schools, faculties, and institutes, underpinning specialized initiatives such as Addressing Global Inequalities, Advanced Materials, and Energy. Connections are further strengthened through our involvement with several research institutes and centres, including the Modelling & Simulation Centre, the Manchester Environmental Research Institute, the Thomas Ashton Institute, the Tyndall Centre for Climate Change Research, the Dalton Nuclear Institute, the Institute for Data Science and Artificial Intelligence, and the Christabel Pankhurst Institute for health technology research and innovation. For more information, please see below: https://www.se.manchester.ac.uk/cem/ https://www.meri.manchester.ac.uk/ https://www.tyndall.manchester.ac.uk/ https://www.dalton.manchester.ac.uk/ https://www.idsai.manchester.ac.uk/ https://www.pankhurst.manchester.ac.uk/ You will be responsible for: Development and delivery of internationally excellent research in the area of civil engineering hydrodynamics, in developing computational and/or experimental programmes. Supervision of postgraduate research students and researchers. Publication of research in leading journals and presentation at international conferences and in other fora relevant to stakeholders and end-users of the research. Undertaking teaching duties (including but not limited to assessment, academic advising and project supervision) within the University on undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Embedding social responsibility and environmental sustainability within teaching and research practice. Contribution to relevant service duties, commensurate with the level of appointment, to support efficient operation of the organisation. Engagement with relevant committees within the School and/or the University. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Educated to PhD level or equivalent in civil engineering hydrodynamics. Demonstrate capability to conduct outstanding research in civil engineering hydrodynamics and/or experience in the development and application of numerical simulation, experimental investigation and data science techniques for civil engineering hydrodynamics. A developing trajectory track record of research output in internationally leading journal publications or other equivalent recognised forms of research output. Ability to lecture to large classes and supervise group and individual projects at Masters and undergraduate level. Good communication skills and an ability to foster interdisciplinary collaboration. Good management and teamwork skills. Commitment to taking responsibility for the health and safety of others, including the development and implementation of risk assessments. Commitment to contributing improvements to advance our inclusive, equal and fair working environment. Desirable Criteria: Capability for establishing links with industry and other academic researchers, generating funding streams from research councils, European Commission and industry. Membership, or intention to become a member, of an appropriate Professional Institution (e.g. ICE, IMechE, IET, etc). We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Benedict Rogers Email: benedict.rogers@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45880": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032225 Salary: £37,694 - £41,064 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Physics & Astronomy Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/10/2026 Contract Duration: 36 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Applications are invited for the position of 36-month PDRA in the Biological Physics Group, Department of Physics and Astronomy, the University of Manchester. We are seeking to appoint an individual with research skills in biointerface and biocolloid to undertake a 36-month research project to study the interactions of biocides with microbial membranes using physical and biochemical approaches including neutron reflection and scattering. The successful applicant is expected to have existing experience in surface and colloidal science, antimicrobial research and the techniques, and a proven track record in publication. The role will report to Prof Jian R Lu. The successful applicant would be a highly motivated individual with proven record in conducting membrane based antimicrobial research and a PhD in biophysics, surface science or equivalent. Along with your application, you should submit a single file containing your CV, list of publications, and research statement (max 2 pages). The Department of Physics and Astronomy is committed to promoting Equality, Diversity, Inclusion and Access through contributing to the University’s social responsibility agenda, demonstrating a commitment to its policies, activities and delivery of initiatives including the Athena SWAN charter for promoting women’s careers in STEMM subjects (science, technology, engineering, mathematics and medicine) in higher education. The Department has held JUNO Champion status since 2016 for its commitment to achieving gender equality which positively promotes inclusivity for all. As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from diverse career paths and backgrounds and from all sections of the community regardless of age, disability, ethnicity, gender, gender expression, sexual orientation and transgender status. Appointment will be made on merit and equal opportunities data completed by applicants will not be accessible by the Recruiting Manager and panel. We support applications from those returning from a career break and are happy to discuss flexible working arrangements. For further information, please visit http://www.physics.manchester.ac.uk/about-us/equality-and-diversity/ (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jian R Lu Email: j.lu@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45963": {
      "title": "Knowledge Transfer Partnership Associate in Advanced and Digitalised Construction",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Knowledge Transfer Partnership Associate in Advanced and Digitalised Construction Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Knowledge Transfer Partnership Associate in Advanced and Digitalised Construction Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032319 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Knowledge Transfer Partnership Associate in Advanced and Digitalised Construction. This 36-month Knowledge Transfer Partnership project with the University of Manchester and Patented Housing ltd will use advanced manufacturing techniques to optimise and digitalise the prefabrication processes of low-cost housing. The role will focus on enhancing efficiency, environmental sustainability, and safety in prefabricated construction. You will be responsible for: Develop simulation models by creating and validating advanced simulation models to replicate dynamic prefabricated construction sites Enhance prefabricated construction processes by identifying areas of inefficiency, safety risks and design mitigation strategies, and align them with industry standards Optimise task allocation by designing strategies to streamline task design and allocation to improve safety, productivity, and environmental sustainability Collaborate with stakeholders by engaging with academics and industry partners to refine models, strategies, and project outputs Disseminate project findings by publishing results in academic journals, generate industry reports, and lead workshops We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Candidates should hold a PhD (or equivalent) in a relevant field such as Mechanical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, Electrical & Electronics Engineering, Computer Science, Digital Construction or a related discipline Demonstrable experience in modelling, simulation, and optimisation of industrial workflows and processes, especially in advanced manufacturing and/or prefabricated construction Proficiency in systems design and optimisation, programming, and practical implementation, with a focus on advanced manufacturing in construction Strong skills in simulation methodologies Excellent communication and interpersonal skills Desirable Criteria: Familiarity with machine learning algorithms and artificial intelligence as applied to advanced manufacturing Understanding of organisational psychology principles related to advanced manufacturing and prefabricated construction Knowledge of construction workflows, equipment, and safety protocols Familiarity with IoT devices, digital twinning, and sensors for advanced manufacturing applications in construction Experience in mentoring or supervising workers, junior researchers, or students We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Akilu Yunusa-Kaltungo and Dr Clara Cheung Email Address: General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45733": {
      "title": "The University of Manchester",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Cyber Ecosystem Project Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Cyber Ecosystem Project Manager Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032226 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Computer Science Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/09/2026 Contract Duration: 9 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Cyber Ecosystem Project Manager. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to managing all operational aspects of the University’s involvement in the Digital Innovation Security Hub (DISH) project, ensuring key project milestones are met including, but not restricted to, DiSH 'OKRS', and coordinating successful delivery to interested parties in Cyber Ecosystem programmes and projects, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: liaising on behalf of University of Manchester with other Cyber Ecosystem organisations (including, but not restricted to, GMCA, Manchester City Council, University of Lancaster, University of Salford and Manchester Metropolitan University, Plexal, and Barclays Eagle Labs). Develop and help maintain excellent communication between all members of the research project, researchers, academics and SMEs. Contribute to project strategy and planning. As Project Manager for the UoM involvement with DiSH, the postholder will also work with: The Digital Futures Communications and Engagement Coordinator to develop themes associated with the Centre for Digital Trust and Security. Cyber Ecosystem programmes and projects including, but not restricted to Cyber Focus, Cyber Com, North West Cyber Corridor, and the Centre for Digital Innovation. Reporting to the School Research and Project Manager and Professor Daniel Dresner, Professor of Cyber Security, the Cyber Ecosystem Manager will be responsible for the administration of the project. The post holder will ensure tasks are carried out in a timely manner with a high level of professionalism, confidentiality and in accordance with all UoM policies and procedures. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: The ability to deliver the requirements of complex projects to strict deadlines together with successful outcomes. Experience of budget planning and financial management skills for large projects. Proven experience of coordinating with multiple organisations and ensuring effective communication, knowledge elicitation, sharing of objectives and resources. Have highly developed inter-personal skills necessary to build and maintain effective working relationships with a wide range of academic staff, external partners and industry stakeholders and to negotiate conflicting views. Desirable Criteria: Prince II or similar project management qualification. Event management experience. Sensitive to the challenges of inter-institutional negotiations and possible conflicts of interest. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Claire Huyton Email Address: Claire.huyton@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032226 Cyber Ecosystem Project Officer FPs 1 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46016": {
      "title": "The University of Manchester",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Internal Communications and Engagement Manager (Maternity Cover) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Internal Communications and Engagement Manager (Maternity Cover) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032359 Salary: £47,389 to £58,225 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Comms, Marketing and Student Recruitment Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/09/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: Directorate of Comms, Marketing & Student Recruitment Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Internal Communications team leads the development and delivery of strategic messaging and campaigns that drive understanding and engagement across the University’s key priorities and programmes. Supporting the delivery of the University’s strategy, we help ensure our internal audiences feel informed, involved and valued. We work with leaders to communicate clearly and consistently, create opportunities for two-way dialogue, and build communication capability across the organisation. Alongside this, we plan and deliver engagement activity for university leadership including the President and Vice-Chancellor’s Office, University Executive Team and Professional Services Leadership Team. The postholder will be responsible for the line management and direction of a team of internal communications professionals within the Directorate of Communications, Marketing and Student Recruitment. They will work with the Head of Internal Communications and other stakeholders in the development and delivery of the University’s internal communications and engagement service and strategy, ensuring the production of dynamic communications and campaigns, and making sure colleagues at all levels are engaged, informed, and receiving consistent messages across the University. This includes playing a leading role in the planning, development and delivery of executive and leadership communications for the President and Vice-Chancellor, University Executive and others. They will ensure a culture of service excellence and continuous improvement across the team by putting our colleagues and students at the heart of everything we do, and embedding a positive sense of identity, direction, purpose, and community. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Grace Conlon Email: grace.conlon@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032359 - Internal Communications and Engagement Manager FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    }
  },
